<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Git-CLI-Scanner is designed with a modular architecture, where separate parts handle specific tasks. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
122 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (The first step is executed by the Command-Line Interface that takes the repository path and scanning options from the user. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
123 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Then, the Repository Analyzer checks the target and collects Git information about it. The File Scanner finds all the files that need to be processed and those that can be excluded from the scan, such as binary files. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
124 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Next, the Detection Engine is responsible for matching patterns and rules to find potential results. Moreover, the Context and Entropy Analysis module cross-checks results to improve the results and reduce false-positive entries. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
125 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (The Git History Scanner helps to detect previous exposure of the sensitive information that was detected in a repository. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
126 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (After that, the Filtering and Deduplication module eliminates duplicates and irrelevant results. Then, the Severity Analyzer scores the results to help understand how critical the issue is.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
127 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Finally, the Redaction and Report Generation module prepares and displays the scan result as a terminal output. Overall, these modules form a complete command-line tool that allows for analyzing Git repositories for secrets efficiently.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
